The claim holds as stated. For tori with 2 <= l <= m and lm <= 200, criterion E is true exactly when lm is divisible by 3, 7, 31, 73 or 85. Checking for 127 adds nothing here, because the only multiple of 127 up to 200 is 127 itself, which is prime and so is not a torus. Codes with k > 0 therefore exist at exactly 89 of the 153 possible n = 2lm, where lm is composite and between 4 and 200:
- all 65 multiples of 6 from 12 to 396;
- 18 multiples of 14 that are not multiples of 6 (28, 56, 70, 98, 112, 140, 154, 182, 196, 224, 238, 266, 280, 308, 322, 350, 364, 392);
- 124, 170, 248, 292, 310 and 340.

On every computed torus where criterion E holds (211 of 211), at least one connected class has k > 0. So the column pattern also holds for the connected-code frontier that is plotted. census-data.json has 86 of these columns and no column outside them. The other three, 390, 392 and 396, have no computed torus yet. Stage 4 status reads "running", 83 of 110 tori, current torus 4x48, and these tori are still in the queue. They are not literally running now. Stage 4 has a 14-day stop rule on starting new tori, so they are expected but not guaranteed.

What the claim does not do: it explains only which n can carry points, that is the x-positions of the columns, mostly a regular step-6 grid (65 of 89 columns). It is a proved result, not a fit. It says nothing about how d grows with n, so on its own it is not "a function that explains the regularity" of d against n. After grouping isomorphic tori, the 89 columns hold 151 distinct groups with codes, out of 251.
